    <h1>Lot-to-lot consistency: why the same peptide isn't the same</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Two vials can carry the same name, the same nominal purity and the same certificate format — and still behave differently. The difference is usually not fraud. It is normal process variation that nobody trended.</p>

    <h2>Where variation comes from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Synthesis scale.</strong> A 1&nbsp;g run and a 100&nbsp;g run do not have identical coupling efficiency; impurities scale differently.</li>
      <li><strong>Purification.</strong> Gradient shape, column load and fraction cut points determine which impurities are removed and which survive.</li>
      <li><strong>Counter-ion exchange.</strong> The same peptide as acetate and as TFA differs in mass, solubility and pH in solution.</li>
      <li><strong>Lyophilization.</strong> Cycle changes affect residual moisture, cake structure and reconstitution behaviour.</li>
      <li><strong>Raw materials.</strong> Resin and amino acid suppliers change; a new lot of resin can shift the impurity profile.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>What a purity number hides</h2>
    <p>“98% by HPLC” says the main peak is 98% of the integrated area at one wavelength. It does not tell you what the other 2% is, whether the method resolved related impurities, or whether the same impurity is present in every lot. A lot that is 98% pure with a growing deletion sequence is a different product from a lot that is 98% pure with a stable profile.</p>

    <h2>Four practical controls</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Control</th><th>What it catches</th><th>Effort</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Retention sample per lot</td><td>Enables side-by-side retest and visual comparison later</td><td>Low — ask for it in the contract</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Full chromatogram, not just a number</td><td>Impurity profile changes that purity alone conceals</td><td>Low — require the PDF attachment</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Trend table across lots</td><td>Slow drift in purity, water content or appearance</td><td>Medium — a simple spreadsheet</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Periodic independent retest</td><td>Divergence between supplier COA and reality</td><td>Medium — cost per lot, sample the critical ones</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>Reading a trend, not a certificate</h2>
    <p>Ask for the last several lots of the same SKU and plot purity, water content and appearance against date. Stable numbers with a stable chromatogram shape are reassuring. A purity figure that sits at 98.1, 98.0, 97.9, 97.8 over a year is technically in spec but describes a process moving in one direction. The question to ask is what changed — column, resin, scale or operator.</p>

    <h2>Specifications that actually constrain drift</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Purity by a named method, with a minimum and a defined integration threshold</li>
      <li>Maximum unspecified impurity, not only total purity</li>
      <li>Water content range by Karl Fischer</li>
      <li>Counter-ion form and residual solvent limits where relevant</li>
      <li>Appearance and reconstitution description</li>
      <li>Lot-to-lot comparability statement: the supplier confirms the impurity profile is consistent with the prior lot</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>When a new lot is not interchangeable</h2>
    <p>If your work depends on a specific lot — an assay, a stability study, a reference standard — freeze enough of that lot to finish the work. Treating a new batch as a drop-in replacement is an assumption, not a control. Where interchangeability matters, ask for a comparability package before you switch.</p>

    <div class="ruo">Research use only. This article describes quality-control practice; it makes no claim about any specific compound.</div>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>Buyers ask for a COA. They should ask for three. One clean lot proves a batch; three comparable lots prove a process, and the process is what you are actually buying.</p>

